# Ownership of the merge gate's own configuration, and of every other path
# the Gate file approval workflow treats as a gate file.
#
# Everything under .github/ is covered: the review workflow, the release
# workflow, the pinned review action reference, and this file itself. A change
# to any of them needs an approving review from an account in the approver set
# of .github/workflows/gate-file-approval.yml: thebenignhacker,
# benign-gate-reviewer[bot] or lication-reviewer[bot]. A review from any other
# account is not counted. The github-actions[bot] login is not in the set,
# so no workflow run approves with its own token: benign-gate-reviewer[bot]
# and lication-reviewer[bot] approve as their own GitHub App identities.
#
# Why .github/: the review workflow runs from the pull request head on
# same-repo branches, so without this rule a pull request that weakens the
# reviewing logic is judged by the weakened logic it just introduced.
#
# The path after .github/ is the rest of the gate pattern in
# .github/workflows/gate-file-approval.yml: the script pr-review.yml runs to
# decide what the reviewer is handed. It carries the same rule as .github/:
# an approving review from that workflow's approver set. Keep this list equal
# to that pattern.
#
# These are the ONLY paths in the repository whose changes need an approving
# review from the approver set. Ordinary pull requests are gated by checks
# alone, and that is deliberate: a reviewer who has to approve everything
# approves nothing.

# Changes under these paths are authored by the repository's automation
# identity, never by the code owner: GitHub does not count an author's review
# of their own pull request, so a change authored by the sole code owner would
# have no approval from thebenignhacker to count.

/.github/ @thebenignhacker
/scripts/pr-review-generated-files.mjs @thebenignhacker
